<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config" (on <application> in
  AndroidManifest.xml). RELEASE config; the debug build swaps in its own copy from
  src/debug/res/xml/ (see below).

  WHAT IT ALLOWS: plaintext HTTP to exactly one host, 127.0.0.1, with includeSubdomains off.
  That host is Meshnode's local sync gateway (http://127.0.0.1:7801), which the Sync tab
  OFFERS when the net.meshnode.app package is installed. Everything else keeps cleartext OFF,
  as android:usesCleartextTraffic="false" did before this file existed.

  WHY A FILE AND NOT THE MANIFEST FLAG: usesCleartextTraffic is all-or-nothing, and on
  API 24+ (minSdk here) it is IGNORED once networkSecurityConfig is set — this file is then
  the only cleartext policy. That is also why the debug build needs its own copy: the debug
  manifest placeholder usesCleartextTraffic="true" (for the `tauri android dev` server) no
  longer has any effect on its own.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT GOVERN: this policy binds the Java/Kotlin network stack and WebView. The
  Rust sync engine (reqwest in libapp_lib.so) opens its own sockets and never consults it;
  plaintext there is confined to loopback by sync::validated_base instead.

  Trust anchors are deliberately not configured: an absent <trust-anchors> inherits the
  platform default (system CAs only for targetSdk 24+), so nothing about TLS changes.

  Pinned by meshnode::tests::android_cleartext_is_permitted_only_for_127_0_0_1, which
  asserts this exact element list. Add a host here only with a test change and a reason.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
